<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :rendering_modes %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:rendering_modes) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The three grayscale tiers') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A grayscale screen carries one tier class. It names how many grays the panel prints, and it decides
            both how colors paint and a handful of display details.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--1bit</code>: every gray token paints as a dither pattern of black and white pixels, borders and text included. This is the baseline the other tiers adjust from.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--2bit</code>: tokens still dither, now between four gray tones instead of two. Table and progress sizing follows the interface scale.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--4bit</code>: every gray token paints as a solid, and each border step gets its own shade instead of two steps sharing one. The title bar loses its pixel-font padding tweak and its text stroke thins.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            On all three tiers, color tokens fall back to their gray equivalents, so a hue never reaches a panel
            that cannot print it.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The demo below labels the tier it is rendering on. Switch device in the Device Preview to see the same
            screen on each one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= framework_example_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_example_inner_classes %>">
            <div class="<%= framework_example_wrapper_classes %>">
              <div class="screen">
                <div class="view view--full">
                  <div class="layout layout--col gap--large">
                    <div class="flex flex--row flex--between flex--center-y">
                      <span class="title">Cold chain</span>
                      <span class="label label--outline hidden 1bit:visible">1-bit</span>
                      <span class="label label--outline hidden 2bit:visible">2-bit</span>
                      <span class="label label--outline hidden 4bit:visible">4-bit</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="progress-bar">
                      <div class="content">
                        <span class="label label--small">Bay 1</span>
                        <span class="label label--small">82%</span>
                      </div>
                      <div class="track">
                        <div class="fill" style="width: 82%"></div>
                      </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="progress-bar progress-bar--emphasis-2">
                      <div class="content">
                        <span class="label label--small">Bay 2</span>
                        <span class="label label--small">64%</span>
                      </div>
                      <div class="track">
                        <div class="fill" style="width: 64%"></div>
                      </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="progress-bar progress-bar--emphasis-3">
                      <div class="content">
                        <span class="label label--small">Bay 3</span>
                        <span class="label label--small">38%</span>
                      </div>
                      <div class="track">
                        <div class="fill" style="width: 38%"></div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="title_bar">
                    <%= image_tag plugin_image_path("trmnl--render.svg"), class: "image image--adaptive", alt: "TRMNL Logo" %>
                    <span class="title">Rendering Modes</span>
                    <span class="instance">Grayscale tiers</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: <<~'HTML'.strip
              <!-- Two grays: the fills dither -->
              <div class="screen screen--ogv2 screen--2bit">...</div>

              <!-- Sixteen grays: the fills paint as solids -->
              <div class="screen screen--v2 screen--4bit">...</div>
            HTML
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <% limited_palette_classes = framework_palette_options.map { |palette| palette[:framework_class] }
                                                            .select { |cls| cls.start_with?('screen--color-') && cls != 'screen--color-full' }
                                                            .uniq %>
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The color modes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A color panel carries a palette class in place of a tier class. Two families cover them.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              One class per limited ink set
              (<%= safe_join(limited_palette_classes.map { |cls| tag.code(cls, class: framework_section_code_classes) }, ', ') %>):
              every framework color, grays included, dithers down to that palette's fixed inks.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-full</code> for 12-bit and 24-bit
              displays: every color paints exactly as defined, with no dithering.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The palette chosen for a device decides which class the screen carries, and the class decides how
            everything paints. <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :color_palettes) %> lists every palette and the class
            it maps to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: <<~'HTML'.strip
              <!-- Seven inks: bg--red-60 dithers between the panel's inks -->
              <div class="screen screen--inkplate_6_color screen--color-7a">...</div>

              <!-- Full color: bg--red-60 paints that exact hex -->
              <div class="screen screen--generic_16_9 screen--color-full">...</div>
            HTML
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'How the runtime learns the depth') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Each mode class also sets
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--framework-bit-depth</code>, its paint depth as a
            number. The JavaScript runtime reads that variable and never parses class names, so a palette added
            in CSS needs no JavaScript change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <%# Depths mirror $_screen-paint-depths and $_limited-palette-paint-depth in base/_screen-mode-vars.scss. %>
        <div class="<%= framework_docs_table_wrapper_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_docs_table_card_classes %>">
            <table class="<%= framework_docs_table_classes %>">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Mode class</th>
                  <th class="<%= framework_docs_table_header_cell_classes %>">Published depth</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="<%= framework_docs_table_body_classes %>">
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">No mode class</td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--1bit</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--2bit</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">2</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--4bit</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">4</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">Any limited palette class</td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">4</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm"><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-full</code></td>
                  <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm">12</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The number describes how the screen paints, not the panel's raw capability. A limited palette prints
            solid inks the way 4-bit prints solid grays, so it reports 4. A screen with no mode class reports 1:
            an unstated device is treated as the most constrained one.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A screen with two mode classes reports the one that actually painted:
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--2bit screen--color-7a</code>
            paints palette inks and reports 4.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The runtime reads the number too: pixel-perfect fonts switch off at 4 and up, and the even-width rule
            for item indexes stops at 2 and up. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :framework_runtime) %>, or
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %> to read the variable from your own JavaScript.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Where the numeric variants stop') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Utility classes take three numeric variant prefixes:
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">1bit:</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">2bit:</code>, and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">4bit:</code>. They end at 4-bit because grayscale glass
            ends at 16 levels, so those three cover every grayscale panel.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            No numeric prefix matches a color screen. Target the color modes from SCSS
            instead, with the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-color-palette($id)</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-color-full</code> mixins on
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_mixins) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: <<~'HTML'.strip
              <!-- A dense dither needs a lighter shade to stay readable -->
              <div class="layout bg--gray-65 2bit:bg--gray-75 4bit:bg--gray-70">...</div>
            HTML
          ) %>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <strong>Do not build on <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--8bit</code> or
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--16bit</code>.</strong>
            Both are compatibility aliases scheduled for removal. They render exactly like
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--4bit</code>, except they report their own
            number as the depth instead of 4.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Nothing above 4-bit grayscale needs them: past 16 grays the next step is color, through
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-full</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :responsive) %> covers the full variant grammar, including how bit-depth
            prefixes combine with breakpoints and orientation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Chart series per mode') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Chart series colors follow the mode too. On grayscale tiers and limited palettes, series start at the
            screen's ink and step toward the background.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-full</code> uses a set of distinct
            colors instead: the ink first, then seven hues in a fixed order, tuned separately for light and dark
            backgrounds. A theme's own chart colors still win over both.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_charts) %> reads these colors from JavaScript, and
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %> covers the markup.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
